::JOUR_U01_L02_GP_Q01::[MLA.JOUR.ETH.02] Scenario: A team is publishing a story, but one detail is unverified. What is the most ethical collaboration choice? {
~A. Publish it because one teammate likes it#Review the scenario and the standard. This choice does not best protect factual, ethical, publication-ready journalism.
=B. Remove or verify the detail before publication#Correct. This answer applies the lesson standard to journalism practice.
~C. Let the fastest teammate decide alone#Review the scenario and the standard. This choice does not best protect factual, ethical, publication-ready journalism.
~D. Ignore concerns to avoid conflict#Review the scenario and the standard. This choice does not best protect factual, ethical, publication-ready journalism.
}
::JOUR_U01_L02_GP_Q02::[MLA.JOUR.INT.01] A teammate says, I think the headline exaggerates what our source said. Which response shows active listening? {
=A. You are worried the headline promises more than the source supports, so let us compare it to the quote.#Correct. This answer applies the lesson standard to journalism practice.
~B. You always complain about headlines.#Review the scenario and the standard. This choice does not best protect factual, ethical, publication-ready journalism.
~C. We do not have time to discuss this.#Review the scenario and the standard. This choice does not best protect factual, ethical, publication-ready journalism.
~D. The headline is fine because I wrote it.#Review the scenario and the standard. This choice does not best protect factual, ethical, publication-ready journalism.
}
::JOUR_U01_L02_GP_Q03::[MLA.JOUR.REP.05] Scenario: A team must publish by 2:00 p.m. Which workflow best supports time management? {
~A. Start designing before deciding the topic#Review the scenario and the standard. This choice does not best protect factual, ethical, publication-ready journalism.
=B. Assign roles, set checkpoints, verify facts, edit, and submit before the deadline#Correct. This answer applies the lesson standard to journalism practice.
~C. Let everyone work separately with no deadline checks#Review the scenario and the standard. This choice does not best protect factual, ethical, publication-ready journalism.
~D. Wait until 1:55 p.m. to begin editing#Review the scenario and the standard. This choice does not best protect factual, ethical, publication-ready journalism.
}
::JOUR_U01_L02_GP_Q04::[MLA.JOUR.ETH.02] Scenario: Two teammates disagree about including an unconfirmed quote. What is the best ethical compromise? {
~A. Publish the quote because disagreement wastes time#Review the scenario and the standard. This choice does not best protect factual, ethical, publication-ready journalism.
~B. Delete the whole story immediately#Review the scenario and the standard. This choice does not best protect factual, ethical, publication-ready journalism.
~C. Use the quote without naming the source#Review the scenario and the standard. This choice does not best protect factual, ethical, publication-ready journalism.
=D. Hold the quote until it is confirmed and publish only verified information#Correct. This answer applies the lesson standard to journalism practice.
}
::JOUR_U01_L02_GP_Q05::[MLA.JOUR.ETH.02] Which statement best explains collaboration in journalism? {
~A. Teamwork means agreeing with the loudest person.#Review the scenario and the standard. This choice does not best protect factual, ethical, publication-ready journalism.
=B. Collaboration means dividing work, listening, solving problems, and protecting accuracy.#Correct. This answer applies the lesson standard to journalism practice.
~C. Collaboration means publishing whatever gets finished first.#Review the scenario and the standard. This choice does not best protect factual, ethical, publication-ready journalism.
~D. Teamwork matters less than making the page look interesting.#Review the scenario and the standard. This choice does not best protect factual, ethical, publication-ready journalism.
}
